# The chat application

NMesh's reference messaging app, built on the data connector. A real end-to-end
messenger: **profiles** (name, bio, avatar), 1:1 **conversations** and
**groups**, **replies**, **editing**, **deletion**, **emoji reactions**,
**delivered/read receipts**, a **typing indicator**, **file and media sharing**
(chunked + verified, images previewed), **voice messages** (Opus), **finding
people by name** (the node's
signed pseudos, whole or partial), plus the **real-time stream** of timestamped
frames —
the primitive a voice/video call would use. Everything stays **E2E encrypted**;
the node sees nothing but routing metadata.

## Built in by default (console `/chat`)

Chat is the **default app** shipped with the node. `scripts/nmesh_node.py`
starts it automatically: it opens a loopback data connector, plugs a `ChatApp`
in internally (section `CHAT_APP_ID`), and surfaces it on the web console at
**`/chat`** (the **Apps** list → **Open**). Nothing to launch: the app is built
in, reachable from the UI, and reuses the console session. `--no-chat` turns it
off. Being shipped with the node (its code is in the repository), it uses a
reserved app_id and is **not signed** — unlike an app deployed over the mesh
(see `Docs/AppSharing/guide`). Console details: `Docs/WebConsole/guide`.

## Self-contained demo

```bash
python scripts/chat_demo.py
```

Brings up two nodes on localhost and runs a conversation, a file transfer (1 MB)
and a real-time stream, with a latency/throughput report. For example:

```
[file]  Bob received : picture.bin (1000000 bytes) integrity=OK
        transfer     : 25 ms  (37.6 MB/s)
[real-time stream — a call]
        frames       : 150/150 at ~50 fps (320 B each)
        latency ms   : avg 0.5 | p50 0.5 | p95 0.8 | max 0.9
```

## Interactive client

Against a node exposing a connector (see `Docs/DataConnector/guide` and the
launcher in `Docs/ProcessLauncher/guide`):

```bash
NMESH_CONNECTOR_HOST=127.0.0.1 NMESH_CONNECTOR_PORT=8790 \
NMESH_CONNECTOR_TOKEN=<token> python scripts/chat_app.py --peer <node_id_hex>
```

Commands: `<text>` sends a message; `/peer <hex>` changes recipient;
`/file <path>` sends a file; `/quit` leaves.

### Web interface (an option of the chat app)

```bash
... python scripts/chat_app.py --peer <hex> --web 8791
# web UI: http://127.0.0.1:8791/   token=<token>
```

With `--web` on, **the chat app** (not the node, not the management console)
pushes its messages to a small web page and routes what you send through the
app. Everything goes through the chat app: the web UI subscribes to its event
stream (`ChatApp.add_listener`). Loopback + Bearer token, strict CSP,
same-origin assets.

## Library

```python
from src.apps.chat import ChatApp, TextMessage, FileReceived, Frame

app = ChatApp(connector_client)      # a ConnectorClient already connected
await app.start()
await app.send_text(peer, "hello")
await app.send_file(peer, "photo.png", data)         # chunked, SHA-256 integrity
await app.send_frame(peer, stream_id, seq, payload)  # real time
ev = await app.next_event()          # TextMessage | FileReceived | Frame
```

## Application format

Every E2E DATA payload: `type(1) ‖ body`.

Every visible message carries a 16-byte **`msg_id`** (minted by the sender) so
replies, edits, deletions, reactions and receipts can reference it. A
**conversation scope** is a 16-byte `group_id`, or all-zero for a direct (1:1)
exchange with the sender.

| Type           | Body                                                          |
|----------------|---------------------------------------------------------------|
| `TEXT`         | msg_id(16), reply_to(16), utf-8 text                          |
| `FILE_OFFER`   | msg_id(16), reply_to(16), transfer_id, total, size, sha256, name |
| `FILE_CHUNK`   | transfer_id, index, data                                      |
| `STREAM`       | stream_id, seq, ts_ns, payload                                |
| `PROFILE`      | bio, avatar (announces my profile — **no name**, see below)    |
| `GROUP_INVITE` | group_id(16), name, roster (a list of node ids)               |
| `GROUP_TEXT`   | group_id(16), msg_id(16), reply_to(16), text                  |
| `RECEIPT`      | kind (delivered/read), scope(16), a list of msg_id            |
| `TYPING`       | scope(16), state (typing / stopped)                           |
| `EDIT`         | scope(16), msg_id(16), new text                               |
| `DELETE`       | scope(16), msg_id(16)                                         |
| `REACTION`     | scope(16), msg_id(16), emoji (empty removes it)               |

### How much of it one peer may send

Chat is the most exposed application on a node: any authenticated member can
send it anything, unasked. Every field was bounded in *size* and nothing was
bounded in *rate*, so one member could hold the receive loop with profile
updates for as long as it liked, and nothing anywhere counted.

Each type now draws on its own allowance per sender (`_LIMITS`, ten-second
windows), and the ceilings are **per kind rather than one bucket** because these
messages are not comparable: a typing notice is a byte and arrives constantly, a
file offer allocates a reassembly buffer and should arrive rarely, a media frame
is the highest-rate thing on the mesh and throttling it breaks a call. A single
shared ceiling would have to be set for the loudest of them — which hands that
same allowance to the expensive ones, so the two that actually cost something
would never be reached.

| Kind | Per 10 s | Weight |
|---|---|---|
| messages, group texts | 40 | 1 |
| reactions, read receipts | 60 | 1 |
| edits, deletions, typing notices | 30 | 1 |
| call frames | 3000 | 1 |
| file chunks | 2000 | 1 |
| file offers | 8 | 2 |
| group invitations | 10 | 3 |
| profile updates | 5 | 3 |

Over the ceiling, chat **drops the message and reports the sender** to the node
(`AppGuard`, `src/app_guard.py`), once per spent window rather than per refused
message. The weight says what the breach is worth: flooding text is rude and
might be a stuck client; flooding profile updates or group invitations is not
something a correct client does at all, so it says more about the sender. What
the reports add up to, and what happens next, is the node's —
[`Docs/Architecture/security.md`](../Architecture/security.md).

Solidity: every application message is validated and bounded; a malformed one is
ignored, never fatal (the charter applies at app level too). File integrity is
verified (SHA-256) before delivery. The sensitive primitives are guarded: a
**reaction/edit/deletion** only acts if it comes from the message's original
sender; a peer's `EDIT`/`DELETE` only ever alters its own messages. A profile
(bio/avatar) is **learned** from the peer; the **name is not in it** — that
comes from the node's signed claim, and the node_id stays the real identity.

## Voice messages

The microphone button (shown when the text box is empty and the browser can
record Opus) records with `MediaRecorder` as **Opus** in WebM or Ogg
(`audio/webm;codecs=opus`, `audio/ogg;codecs=opus`, 32 kbit/s, five minutes at
most) and sends it as an ordinary file transfer — chunked, verified, rate
limited like any other. There is no new wire type: a voice message is a file
whose name says so, `voice-<milliseconds>.webm|ogg`. The name is the sender's,
so the receiving node decides (`chat_web.voice_length`): the pattern must match
exactly, the length is 1 ms to 10 minutes and the file 1 byte to 8 MiB, the size
must be a plain integer — anything else is shown as the plain file it is, and a
non-Opus file with a borrowed name is the browser's to refuse. The page fetches
the bytes through its authenticated `GET /api/chat/file` (so it follows the node
being driven), plays them from a blob URL (at most 24 held) and shows a play
button, a seek bar and the length. A browser that cannot record Opus simply has
no microphone button, and still plays what it receives.

## The screen

Three panes on one grid: the **conversation list**, the **thread**, and the node
card of whoever you are talking to. The list has two tabs: **Chats** and
**Contacts** (the mirror above, one tap to start a conversation, a link to the
Contacts page). Which of them is on screen is a single
attribute on the shell — `data-view="list" | "conv" | "peer"` — so at any width
there is exactly one answer to "what am I looking at", and the panes cannot
contradict each other.

Wide enough (over 1180 px) shows all three. Between 860 and 1180 the node card
takes the conversation's place, because everything in it is *about* the
conversation. Below 860 the screen shows one pane at a time and the list is the
navigation — no drawer and no hamburger, the same stance the console takes with
its tab bar.

A touch screen (`pointer:coarse`) gets larger targets, decided by the pointer
and not by a user-agent string; the composer swaps its microphone for the send
button as soon as there is text, and shows only the recorder (cancel, time,
send) while recording, so a tap cannot send half a sentence typed beside it.
Every sentence is read from the catalogue — `Docs/I18n/guide`.

Everything is prefixed `ch-`. The design system (`src/webassets/ui.py`) owns the
generic names, and this page used to redefine `.msg` — quietly changing a shared
component for itself alone. The prefix is what stops that.

Three rules hold the layout together, and each of them is a bug that was really
here (see `Docs/WebConsole/design` → *Constraints you cannot see*):

- every grid track is `minmax(0,1fr)`. A bare `1fr` sizes to its content's
  min-content width, and one pasted URL then pushed the page 126 px wider than
  a phone;
- the log's children are `flex:none`. Without it the browser shrank every bubble
  instead of scrolling — messages crushed to 20 px and overlapping;
- the message width cap sits on the row, which has a definite width, not on the
  bubble inside a row that was itself shrink-to-fit.

Both lists are **keyed**: the page polls every 1.2 s, and a row is created once,
updated in place, and removed when it goes. Rebuilding them threw away the
scroll position, any selection, and the click somebody was in the middle of
making.

The poll reads through the console's feed (`src/console_feed.py`), which
decides three things for it. A read that is not an answer — a 502 carries a
body too, and that body has no contacts in it — hands back **nothing**, and the
page keeps the conversation list it had: it was true a moment ago and an error
is not a newer truth. The social half travels as named **sections** and only
when it moved, instead of the whole contact list arriving beside each message.
And every answer names the build that gave it, so a node that updated itself
under an open page is noticed once and reloaded, rather than found out through
a run of questions it no longer answers. The messages stay outside the
sections — they are already asked for by sequence. A message that arrives while you are reading further up does not yank
the view — it offers a **New messages ↓** button instead.

## Who you are talking to (the details panel)

The name at the top of a conversation opens **the console's node card** — the
same view everywhere (`src/webassets/nodeview.py`): the real link, its
addresses, and what each of the two nodes is allowed to do to the other. Chat
does not re-describe it, it **mounts** it; this is not an iframe, it is the same
code in the same document, so no page needs to be frameable.

The "Message" button is not there: you are already there. The fleet buttons are
— asking for rights on the machine you are talking to therefore takes no leaving
the conversation.

Where it appears is the user's choice (profile → Preferences): **beside the
conversation** (the default), in a **window**, or in a **tab**. Stored in this
browser, like the theme.

## The social layer (contacts, groups, search)

All of it lives **in the app** (the node knows nothing of these notions) and is
persisted by `src/apps/chat_state.py` (`ChatState`, bounded). The built-in chat
puts it in the node's **encrypted drawer** (`node.app_storage`, section
`CHAT_APP_ID`, through `DrawerStore`): contacts and the names they go by are
**never in the clear on disk**, and survive a restart. **Message history** (the web UI's feed,
`ChatBridge`) is persisted in the same place → it no longer vanishes on a
refresh or a restart. Without `--data`, the drawer stays in RAM (demos/tests). A
`ChatState(path=…)` in plain JSON remains available for tests and demos with no
real data.

- **My identity**: `whoami` gives my node id (to copy and share). My **name is
  the node's**, not chat's: the *Display name* field in the settings dialog
  writes it through the console (`POST /api/pseudo`), the node signs a claim for
  it, and chat mirrors it back with the rest. Chat's own profile is the **bio
  and avatar**, which it does announce to contacts (`PROFILE`).
- **Contacts**: chat has **no address book of its own**. The people it shows are
  the [contacts app's](contacts), read and written through the permission a
  person gave chat (`contacts.read` / `contacts.write`, `Docs/AppPermissions/guide`).
  `ChatBridge.contacts_access()` is one of `write`, `read`, `denied` or
  `unavailable`, and the page is built for each: *denied* shows a permission card
  (*Allow*, *Read only*, *Not now*) and the contacts chat already had stay on
  screen as they were — a node that has not answered loses nothing; the first
  time chat may **write**, those contacts move into the default book (once,
  `migrated`); afterwards the list chat renders from is a **mirror** of the books
  (`ChatState.replace_contacts`), carrying the name its owner gave each node
  (`label`), refreshed on every poll. `add_contact(id)` saves the node in the
  books (named from the directory) and announces my profile to them; refused it
  raises `PermissionError(permission)`, which the console returns as `403
  {"error":"permission","permission":…}` so the page asks the question instead of
  failing. Starting a chat does **not** save the person — a conversation and an
  address book are two decisions — and any conversation shows **Add to
  contacts** while the node is in no book.
- **Groups**: `create_group(name, members)` mints a `group_id`, adds me to the
  roster and sends a `GROUP_INVITE` to each member. `send_group_text` **fans**
  the message out to every member (members never relay → no loop, no
  amplification). The mesh has no multicast: a group is purely an application
  notion.
- **Names come from the node, always.** `refresh_pseudos` re-reads them from the
  node every `_NAMES_REFRESH` seconds, and on demand; the node holds the signed
  claims, chat only mirrors them. A name never arrives in a chat message: an
  unsigned pseudo in a peer-to-peer profile is a peer telling us what to call
  it, which is exactly how impersonation works. That field is gone, and so are
  the old `DIR_QUERY` / `DIR_REPLY` messages it fed.
- **Search by name** — `search_pseudo(query, wide=…)`: saved contacts first
  (found by the name their owner gave them, `match = -1`), then the node's own
  ranked search (exact, then prefix, then a word inside the name, then anywhere;
  case- and accent-insensitive), merged with `find_by_pseudo` over the contacts
  already on this screen. `wide=True` also asks the network for the exact name,
  which finds somebody this node has never met. See
  [`Docs/Pseudos/guide`](../Pseudos/guide).

All of this is drivable from the console's `/chat` sub-page (identity + copying
the id, contacts, groups, search). See `Docs/WebConsole/guide`. `/chat#c/<node id>`
opens (or starts) the conversation with that node; the Contacts page and the
node card link to it.

## Audio calls (`src/apps/call.py`)

The `STREAM` flow is a call's primitive. `AudioCall` builds on it to carry PCM
audio in real time:

```bash
python scripts/call_demo.py      # 2 nodes, a WAV streamed, audio verified bit for bit
```

```python
from src.apps.call import AudioCall, WavSource, WavSink
tx = AudioCall(chat_app)
await tx.place(peer, WavSource("in.wav"), stream_id=1)   # sends
rx = AudioCall(chat_app); rx.attach()                    # receives
rx.render(src, 1, WavSink("out.wav"))
```

The **WAV** backend (stdlib `wave`) gives an end-to-end testable call with real
samples, with no hardware and no dependency. For a **live mic/speaker**,
implement `AudioSource`/`AudioSink` with a device backend (sounddevice, say)
**in your app** — NMesh keeps no audio dependency.
